      <related-faq>
         <name>What happens during an emergency for individuals who require assistance?</name>
         <showProductCollection></showProductCollection>
         <showLicenseCollection></showLicenseCollection>
         <organization></organization>
         <faq>
            <calltakerOnly></calltakerOnly>
            <keywords>EEAP, Disabled Evacuees, residents with disabilities, special needs, Emergency evacuation
               assistance, shelter services, service animals</keywords>
            <priority></priority>
            <Jurisdiction_511>
               <jurisdiction>local</jurisdiction>
               <localType>county</localType>
               <county>miami-dade</county>
               <municipality></municipality>
               <organization>org1665164800368109</organization>
               <subOrganization></subOrganization>
               <parentService></parentService>
            </Jurisdiction_511>
            <text>What happens during an emergency for individuals who require assistance?</text>
            <displayTitle>Emergency Evacuation Assistance Program for special needs  </displayTitle>
            <about>Special accommodations through the Emergency Evacuation Assistance Program are available
               to evacuees with special needs. </about>
            <answer>&lt;p&gt;Individuals enrolled in the Emergency Evacuation Assistance Program (EEAP) will
               be contacted by phone, text or other method of communication to coordinate assistance
               when an evacuation order is issued to determine what specialized sheltering and/or
               transportation services are needed. You will be informed which facility you will be
               evacuated to and a time frame for your pick-up.&lt;/p&gt;
               &lt;p&gt;It is the EEAP client&amp;rsquo;s or caregiver/companion&amp;rsquo;s responsibility to
               monitor the media for updated information on what actions they may need to take. The
               individual and caregiver/companion must also be prepared to evacuate when transportation
               arrives along with their readiness kits. If they miss their scheduled pickup, there
               may not be time for a second attempt.&lt;/p&gt;
               &lt;p&gt;All EEAP clients must bring the following in the event of an evacuation:&lt;/p&gt;
               &lt;ul&gt;
               &lt;li&gt;You must be accompanied at all times by a caregiver or companion&lt;/li&gt;
               &lt;li&gt;Comfort items in one carrier (e.g. pillows, linens, blankets, cot, air mattress,
               sleeping bag, folding chair, change of clothing, toiletries)&lt;/li&gt;
               &lt;li&gt;Minimum one-week (7 day) supply of all your medications and specific medication
               instructions&lt;/li&gt;
               &lt;li&gt;Medical equipment (e.g. oxygen equipment, wheelchair, walking cane, diabetes equipment,
               feeding tubes, dressing supplies)&lt;/li&gt;
               &lt;li&gt;Minimum three (3) day supply of food and water for you and your caregiver/companion
               (special dietary foods, if required)&lt;/li&gt;
               &lt;li&gt;Names and telephone numbers of your emergency contacts and neighbors&lt;/li&gt;
               &lt;/ul&gt;
               &lt;p&gt;To request a Hurricane Readiness Guide in an alternate format, such as Braille
               or large print, call 311 or send an email to &lt;a href="mailto:<a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="32575d51725f5b535f5b565356571c555d44">[email&#160;protected]</a>"&gt;<a href="/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection" class="__cf_email__" data-cfemail="3a5f55597a57535b57535e5b5e5f145d554c">[email&#160;protected]</a>&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
               &lt;center&gt;
               &lt;p&gt;&lt;iframe src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/5AkEFCYyCeI" width="560" height="350"
               frameborder="0" allowfullscreen=""&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
               &lt;/center&gt;</answer>
            <publishDate>2022-10-07</publishDate>
            <pubHour>06</pubHour>
            <pubMin>55</pubMin>
            <pubTod>pm</pubTod>
