      <related-faq>
         <name>Why did I receive a phone call from the EEAP?</name>
         <showProductCollection></showProductCollection>
         <showLicenseCollection></showLicenseCollection>
         <organization></organization>
         <faq>
            <calltakerOnly>true</calltakerOnly>
            <keywords>EEAP, eeap call back, eeap called, calling back eeap, call back, Emergency Evacuation
               Assistance Program, Hurricane, storm approaching, before storm, hurricane, tropical
               storm, evacuation, emergency, covid test kits, facilities,</keywords>
            <priority>call back, eeap called me, calling back eeap, eeap called, </priority>
            <Jurisdiction_511>
               <jurisdiction>local</jurisdiction>
               <localType>county</localType>
               <county>miami-dade</county>
               <municipality></municipality>
               <organization>org1665164800368109</organization>
               <subOrganization></subOrganization>
               <parentService></parentService>
            </Jurisdiction_511>
            <text>Why did I receive a phone call from the EEAP?</text>
            <displayTitle>EEAP: During an Emergency</displayTitle>
            <about>When a hurricane threatens, the County will call all the registrants who qualify for
               evacuation assistance to coordinate pick up and sheltering accommodations.</about>
            <answer>&lt;p&gt;When an emergency threatens the County, EEAP clients may be contacted via phone,
               text or other method of communication to coordinate transportation and sheltering
               accommodations, as well as receive important notifications.&lt;/p&gt;
               &lt;p&gt;Individuals may also be contacted throughout the year to verify that all contact
               and medical information is current.&lt;/p&gt;
               &lt;p&gt;Automated call downs may also be programmed to send important messages to residential
               healthcare facilities who have emergency plans submitted to the County's Emergency
               Management Department.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</answer>
            <publishDate>2022-10-07</publishDate>
            <pubHour>06</pubHour>
            <pubMin>50</pubMin>
            <pubTod>pm</pubTod>
            <call311></call311>
            <call711></call711>
            <category>
               <mainCategory>public-safety</mainCategory>
               <subCategory>hurricane</subCategory>
               <subTopics>emergency-evacuation-assistance-eeap</subTopics>
            </category>
            <faqCategory>
               <mainCategory></mainCategory>
            </faqCategory>
            <serviceRequestURL>
               <title></title>
               <description></description>
               <buttonText></buttonText>
               <link>
            </serviceRequestURL>
            <slug>why-did-i-receive-a-phone-call-from-the-eeap</slug>
            <mduid>faq1598014015110910</mduid>
            <organizationName>Emergency Management</organizationName>
            <subTopicValue></subTopicValue>
            <pubDateTime>2022-10-07T18:50</pubDateTime>
            <subCategoryValue>hurricane</subCategoryValue><script><title>Call Down Message - When no threat exists</title>
               <accordionState></accordionState>
               <content><p>The Department of Emergency Management calls all the EEAP registrants throughout
                  the year to make sure contact information is up-to-date.</p>
                  <p><strong><span style="text-decoration: underline;">311 Agent Instruction:&nbsp;</span>
                  </strong></p>
                  <p>If the caller states that a message from the EEAP was left on their phone, follow
                  the instructions below.</p>
                  <p>The purpose of the call-downs is:&nbsp;</p>
                  <ul>
                  <li>To ask if the registrant wishes to evacuate (in the event an evacuation order
                  is issued)</li>
                  <li>To verify address (location and unit number, if applicable)</li>
                  <li>To verify contact information (phone number(s)</li>
                  </ul>
                  <p>Should you receive a call, send an email with the information below to:&nbsp; <a
                  href="mailto:ESU@miamidade.gov">EEAP@miamidade.gov</a></p>
                  <p>Include the following information (you may copy and paste into the email):</p>
                  <ul>
                  <li><strong>Email Subject Line:</strong>&nbsp; Patient Name and ID number</li>
                  <li>In the body of the email, include:</li>
                  <ul>
                  <li>Patient Name:</li>
                  <li>Address:</li>
                  <li>Unit Number (if applicable):</li>
                  <li>Contact Number:</li>
                  </ul>
                  <li>Does patient wish to remain on the registry?&nbsp; YES or NO</li>
                  <li>Will the patient need transportation assistance during an emergency?&nbsp; &nbsp;
                  <em>YES or NO</em></li>
                  </ul>
                  <p><u>Family member reporting a registrant is deceased, send an email to:</u>&nbsp;&nbsp;<u><a
                  href="mailto:eeap@miamidade.gov">EEAP@miamidade.gov</a>&nbsp;</u><strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;</strong></p>
                  <ul>
                  <li><strong>Email Subject Line:</strong>&nbsp; Patient Name and ID number</li>
                  <li>In the body of the email, include:</li>
                  <ul>
                  <li>Name and Contact Information of the individual informing of a deceased registrant.</li>
                  <ul>
                  <li>(include death date if possible)</li>
                  </ul>
                  <li>Registrants Name:</li>
                  <li>Address:</li>
                  <li>Unit Number (if applicable):</li>
                  <li>Contact Number:</li>
                  <li>Note: Registrant is Deceased</li>
                  </ul>
                  </ul></content>
               <serviceRequestURL>
                  <srType></srType>
               </serviceRequestURL></script><script><title>Call-Down Message - When DEM is Activated</title>
               <accordionState></accordionState>
               <content><p><strong>The Department of Emergency Management (DEM), EEAP conducts a call-down
                  to the program registrants in preparation for a storm.</strong></p>
                  <p><u>If the caller states that a message from the EEAP was left on their phone, follow
                  the below instructions.</u></p>
                  <p><u>The purpose of the call-downs is:&nbsp;</u></p>
                  <ul>
                  <li>To confirm that the registrant is still a resident of Miami-Dade</li>
                  <li>To ask if the registrant wishes to evacuate (in the event an evacuation order
                  is issued)</li>
                  <li>To verify address (location and unit number, if applicable)</li>
                  <li>To verify contact information (phone number(s)</li>
                  </ul>
                  <p><strong>311 Agent:&nbsp; &nbsp;When Instructed to begin updating and entering information
                  in the EEAP Database:</strong></p>
                  <p><u>Instructions:</u></p>
                  <ul>
                  <li>Open the <a href="https://www.miamidade.gov/Apps/GIS/EEAP/main/PSNEntryFormLogIn.aspx"
                  target="_blank" rel="noopener">EEAP Database</a> to search for the client.</li>
                  <ul>
                  <li>Search using Name, Address or Phone Number</li>
                  </ul>
                  <li>If client is found, under the column named "Command Center". Next to the client&rsquo;s
                  name click on "TBD".</li>
                  <li>On the question: "Will Evacuate" select from drop down:</li>
                  <ul>
                  <li>Evacuate - YES</li>
                  <li>Declined - NO</li>
                  </ul>
                  <li>Add additional comments/notes in the "Comments Section"</li>
                  <ul>
                  <li>Example: Daughter answered on client's behalf (time and date of call),</li>
                  <li>Example: _____ is not in town, husband stated she does not want to evacuate (time
                  and date of call)</li>
                  </ul>
                  <li>Click on the calendar to "Stamp Date"</li>
                  <li><strong>Click on "Submit" <span style="color: #e03e2d;">(THIS MUST BE DONE) </span>The
                  system does not save any changes unless you do this.&nbsp;</strong></li>
                  </ul>
                  <p><strong>311 Agent:&nbsp;&nbsp;</strong><u>If unable to find the client on the EEAP
                  Database, please send an email with the below information to:</u> &nbsp;<a href="mailto:ESU@miamidade.gov">ESU@miamidade.gov</a>
                  (email ONLY for use by 311 during activation)&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>
                  <p>Include the following information (you may copy and paste into the email):</p>
                  <ul>
                  <li><strong>Email Subject Line:</strong>&nbsp; Patient Name and ID number</li>
                  <li>In the body of the email, include:</li>
                  <ul>
                  <li>Patient Name:</li>
                  <li>Address:</li>
                  <li>Unit Number (if applicable):</li>
                  <li>Contact Number:</li>
                  </ul>
                  <li>Does patient wish to evacuate?&nbsp; &nbsp;<em>YES or NO</em></li>
                  <li>Does patient need transportation assistance?&nbsp; &nbsp; <em>YES or NO</em></li>
                  <ul>
                  <li>Provide details:&nbsp; &nbsp;<em>(example: wheelchair, ambulance transportation,
                  etc.)</em></li>
                  </ul>
                  <li>What are the medical conditions and severity of the condition?&nbsp;</li>
                  <li>What medical equipment is used by the EEAP Registrant?&nbsp; &nbsp;<em>(ventilator,
                  oxygen dependent, etc.)</em>&nbsp;</li>
                  <li>How often is the medical equipment used?&nbsp; <em>(Intermittent or Continuous)</em></li>
                  </ul>
                  <p><span style="color: #e03e2d;">(this email is for use by 311 only during an "in-storm"
                  event only)&nbsp;&nbsp;</span></p></content>
               <serviceRequestURL>
                  <srType></srType>
               </serviceRequestURL></script><script><title>Hurricane Milton EEAP Clients</title>
               <accordionState></accordionState>
               <content><p><strong>Instructions for EEAP Clients Regarding Potential Evacuation Due to Hurricane
                  Milton</strong></p>
                  <p>In response to Hurricane Milton, the County is considering evacuating residents
                  in Storm Surge Zones A, B, and C who are registered with the Emergency Evacuation
                  and Assistance Program (EEAP).</p>
                  <p><strong>Step 1: Determine Evacuation Needs</strong></p>
                  <p><strong>Do you wish to be evacuated from your home?</strong></p>
                  <ul>
                  <li>Verify all sections of the client&rsquo;s EEAP record, including General Information,
                  Assistance Needed, Medical Conditions, and Home Health Care.</li>
                  <li><strong>Record the client&rsquo;s response</strong> on your call sheet (use the
                  list of responses attached to these instructions).</li>
                  </ul>
                  <p><strong>Step 2: Instructions Based on Client Response</strong></p>
                  <ul>
                  <li><strong>For clients who will drive themselves:</strong><br />Inform them that
                  we will contact them to coordinate their arrival time at their designated shelter,
                  which will be after 9:00 a.m.</li>
                  <li><strong>For clients who require transportation:</strong><br />Let them know that
                  their transportation provider will call on the morning of <strong>Tuesday, October
                  8, 2024</strong>, with their pickup time.</li>
                  </ul>
                  <p><strong>Step 3: Important Reminders for EEAP Clients</strong></p>
                  <ul>
                  <li><strong>Mandatory Evacuation:</strong><br />Clients must evacuate when transportation
                  arrives at their home. If they decline transportation upon arrival, they may be charged
                  for the service.</li>
                  <li><strong>Hospital Accommodations:</strong><br />Remind clients they will not be
                  admitted as hospital patients. Instead, they will be assigned to a designated area
                  within the facility for the duration of the storm. Accommodations may not include
                  a bed.</li>
                  </ul>
                  <p><strong>Step 4: What Clients Must Bring to the Shelter</strong></p>
                  <ol>
                  <li>A <strong>caregiver</strong> must accompany the client at all times.</li>
                  <li><strong>Bedding supplies</strong>, such as pillows, linens, blankets, a cot, air
                  mattress, or folding chair.</li>
                  <li>A <strong>7-day supply</strong> of all medications, along with specific medication
                  instructions.</li>
                  <li>Any <strong>medical equipment</strong> needed (e.g., wheelchair, walker, oxygen,
                  feeding equipment, diapers).</li>
                  <li>A <strong>3-day supply</strong> of snacks and water.</li>
                  <li><strong>Names and contact information</strong> for emergency contacts and neighbors.</li>
                  <li><strong>Cash</strong> for personal expenses.
                  <p><strong>Step 5: Stay Informed</strong></p>
                  <ul>
                  <li>Advise clients to stay tuned to local news for updates.</li>
                  <li>For further information, they can call <strong>3-1-1</strong>.</li>
                  </ul>
                  <p><strong>Step 6: Additional Instructions</strong></p>
                  <ul>
                  <li><strong>Verify Address and Transportation Mode</strong><br />Ensure the client&rsquo;s
                  address and transportation details are correct.</li>
                  <li><strong>If the client declines evacuation:</strong><br />Remind them that we cannot
                  guarantee pickup later if they change their mind. If they experience a medical emergency
                  or power loss during the storm, they must call 911. Be aware that once tropical storm
                  force winds reach 40 mph, ambulances will no longer be able to respond.</li>
                  <li><strong>Offer a follow-up call</strong> after the storm to check on their well-being.</li>
                  </ul>
                  <p><strong>Documentation</strong></p>
                  <ul>
                  <li><strong>Record your initials, date, and time</strong> as well as any important
                  notes on the PSN Information Form.</li>
                  </ul>
                  <p>&nbsp;</p>
                  </li>
                  </ol></content>
               <serviceRequestURL>
                  <srType></srType>
               </serviceRequestURL></script></faq>
      </related-faq>
      <slug>emergency-evacuation-assistance</slug>
